Sports Non-Verbal Communication Video Annotator  

# A lightweight web app for tagging, analyzing, and exporting non-verbal communication events in sports footage.

# Features/Controls:
- Upload new video intially or to replace current video
- play/pause button  
- Click to play/pause on the video  
- Progress bar  
- Volume control (mute + slider)  
- Playback speed controls (0.25× → 2×)  
- Keyboard Shortcuts:
  - Spacebar: Pause/Play
  - Left/Right Arrow: Jump forward/backward 3 seconds



# Annotation System
- Predefined non-verbal communication action buttons  
- Custom annotations (“Other”)  
- Automatic timestamping (video time + optional game clock)  
- Click an annotation to jump to that moment  
- Delete any annotation  
- Export all annotations as `annotations.json`  



# Tagging Panels and Buttons
- Click the pencil on a button (or right-click it) to edit it: label, definition, color, group, hotkey, lead/lag, descriptors, and delete
- Any button can be deleted. An Undo link shows up for a few seconds
- "Create New Button" adds one button. "Bulk add" takes a list separated by commas, semicolons or new lines and skips names that already exist
- Colors come from a palette or any hex value
- Drag buttons to reorder them, or drop one on a group header to move it into that group. Click a group header to collapse it
- Hotkeys: set a single key per button in the editor, then press it to tag. They are off while you type or while a dialog is open
- Lead/lag: a button can save a range (tag time minus lead, plus lag) instead of a single time. Exports have Start and End columns
- Descriptors: options that are asked after you tag, like "Teammate, Official"
- Panels: keep several sets of buttons and switch between them with the dropdown. You can create, rename, duplicate, delete and reset them, and export or import them as JSON. Import adds new panels and never overwrites yours
- Panels and the annotations of each video are saved in the browser (localStorage)

# More Annotation Features
- Edit an annotation with the pencil: label, time, game clock, descriptor and a note
- Filter the list by action or team, and use "Play clips" to watch the filtered annotations one after another
- A timeline under the player shows one row per action. Click a marker to jump to it
- Summary shows counts per action per team and can be exported as CSV
- A single video can be uploaded from the start screen without picking a folder
- The game clock can be typed in the Game Clock box and is added to each new annotation

# Game Clock and Scoreboard
- The game clock and the score are read from the video, so you don't have to type them
- Click "Set scoreboard area" and draw a box around the scoreboard. It is looked at straight away: the app compares a few moments of the video, finds the parts that stay in place, and works out the team names, the two scores and the clock. The team names go on the Team buttons. The box can include the clock or not
- It keeps the clock and scores up to date while the video plays and after you seek ("Live read"). If the scores can't be read for a while it looks at the scoreboard again, in case the layout changed. "Re-detect" does it on demand
- If it can't work it out, open "Manual options" and draw the boxes yourself: "Set score A" and "Set score B" around each score number, and "Set clock area" around the clock. "Find clock" tries to find the clock by itself
- "Auto on tag" reads the clock and scores for every new tag and saves them on the annotation
- If the clock moves around the screen, tick "Camera moves" and set the clock area again at a few points in the video
- Readings are checked before they are used. The clock can't run faster than the video or change direction, and a score can only go up by a small amount and has to be seen twice. This stops glitches like 0 turning into 108. When you jump to another part of the video the boxes are cleared until a new reading is found, and if the scoreboard is hidden for a moment the last good value stays
- The small box next to the clock is a period (like Q2) that gets added to the clock
- Reading uses tesseract.js, which downloads its data the first time, so it needs an internet connection. Results can be wrong, so check them
- The exports get a Scoreboard column and a scores field

# Definitions:

- Eye Contact  
 - Intentional glance used to signal:
  - Readiness for a pass  
  - Acknowledgement of a defensive change  
  - Confirmation of a play or cut  

- Screen  
 - Indicates a screen is coming. Includes:
  - Moving toward a defender to set a pick  
  - Using a stance or gesture to signal where the screen will be  

- Directing (Gesturing / Pointing)  
 - Hand motions or body cues used to direct teammates:
  - Where to move  
  - When to cut  
  - Which play is being executed  

- Call for Ball  
  - Non-verbal gestures asking for a pass:
  - Open hand raised  
  - Hand extended toward passer  
  - Flashing open into space  

- Defensive Switch  
 - Silent defensive coordination:
  - Pointing at a new assignment  
  - Gesturing to swap matchups  
  - Eye contact + hand signal to change coverage  

- Nod  
 - A subtle confirmation cue:
  - “Run it now”  
  - “I’m ready”  
  - “I see the same read as you”  

- Other (Custom)  
 - User-defined events not covered above.  
  - Examples:
   - “Hand wave”  
   - “Tap on chest”  
   - “Backdoor signal”  



# Annotation Controls
 - Action Buttons: Add an annotation with that label 
  - Game Clock Input: Optional text like “10:45 Q2” 
  - Custom Annotation (“Other”): Enter custom label + click “Add Other” 
  - Annotation List: Shows events newest → oldest 
  - Click Annotation: Jump to annotation time 
  - Trash Icon: Delete annotation 
  - Export: Download "annotations.json" or whatever the user names the file



# Export Format
- Exports an array of JSON objects like:

 - json
{
  "id": 1732461123456,
  "type": "Screen",
  "label": "Screen",
  "timestamp": 32.841,
  "formattedTime": "0:32",
  "gameClockTime": "9:12 Q1" (if applicable),
  "activeTeamName": "Team A",
  "startTime": 32.841,   (timestamp minus the button's lead)
  "endTime": 32.841,     (timestamp plus the button's lag)
  "descriptor": "Teammate" (if used),
  "note": ""
}

 - csv

Timestamp,Game Clock,Team,Action,Label,Start,End,Descriptor,Note
(the first five columns are unchanged)




# How to Run

To run the annotator locally: 

 - 1. Install Dependencies
  - Have **Node.js** installed (version 16+ recommended).  

 - 2. Then, in the project folder, run:
  npm install

 - 3. Start the Development Server
  Run: npm start

- This will start the app at:
  
   http://localhost:3000



